INT. SUBURBAN KITCHEN - NIGHT

Dark. Candles. A power cut. Pots on the stove, stone cold.

WINIFRED LAWSON, 60s, stirs a bowl of soup by torchlight. Her husband ARTHUR LAWSON, 60s, kneels at the fuse box. Their kid RIVER LAWSON, 20s, they/them, scrolls a dead phone. Their neighbour DOLORES FINCH, 70s, has wandered in with a casserole.

THE CHILDREN, three nephews in matching pyjamas, sit on the counter in a row.

ARTHUR
It isn't the fuse. It isn't the breaker, it isn't the meter, it is the entire street, and I have told the electricity company four times that the grid in this neighbourhood has been held together by optimism since 1987.

WINIFRED
Arthur.

ARTHUR
(beat)
I'm saying that when we moved here, there was a man who came round and said, don't worry about the cables, they're very modern. Modern! They've got moss on them.

DOLORES
Mine are fine.

ARTHUR
Yours is on the other transformer, Dolores. You're a different civilisation.

River holds up the black phone.

RIVER
Dead.

WINIFRED
Then talk to us. We're the next best thing.

THE CHILDREN
(in unison)
We're hungry.

Candle flicker. A fridge hum, absent.

River's phone lights up, wakes, flickers. 1 percent.

INSERT - PHONE
ON PHONE: mum: are u ok?? power's out everywhere

RIVER
(smiling)
Mum's texting. From the next room.

WINIFRED
She is not.

River turns the screen round. Winifred reads. Laughs. The lights snap on. Everyone blinks. Arthur stands there with a screwdriver, triumphant, wrong.
